{"reference":"1 Samuel 28","book":"1 Samuel","chapter":28,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The Figure at Endor","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The narrator identifies the figure who appears at Endor as Samuel. Readers have understood the scene in different ways: as an exceptional appearance permitted by God, as an apparition produced by the medium, or through another literary-theological explanation. The chapter itself presents the encounter without settling that interpretive question.","verse_refs":["1 Samuel 28:3-25"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_samuel_028_f8ea871bfab6326cc4a2841d"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"In the Larger Book","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"First Samuel’s larger movement runs from Eli’s failing priestly house through Samuel’s prophetic leadership and Saul’s rejected kingship, then toward David’s anointing, rise, exile, and Saul’s death. This chapter belongs within that continuing account.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_samuel_028_50a4eb2ff6927e5571cdc9be"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}